{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Chasing Cherry Blossoms in Kyoto**\n\nMy recent trip to Japan was a whirlwind of ancient traditions and futuristic wonders. Landing in Tokyo, I was immediately struck by the neon glow of Shibuya Crossing, a chaotic yet perfectly synchronized dance of pedestrians. The energy was electric, fueling my excitement for the days ahead.\n\nHowever, the true magic happened when I boarded the Shinkansen to Kyoto. Stepping off the bullet train, the pace slowed instantly. I spent my mornings wandering through the Fushimi Inari Shrine, hiking up the mountain path lined with thousands of vibrant orange torii gates. The silence between the crowds felt sacred, broken only by the rustling of bamboo in the Arashiyama Grove nearby.\n\nOne afternoon, I stumbled upon a small tea house tucked away in the Gion district. An elderly woman served me matcha and wagashi sweets with a grace that spoke of decades of practice. Sitting on the tatami mats, watching cherry blossom petals drift onto the garden pond, I felt a profound sense of peace. It wasn't just about seeing the sights; it was about feeling the moment.\n\nEvenings were reserved for culinary adventures. From steaming bowls of ramen in narrow alleyways to exquisite kaiseki dinners, every meal was an art form. The hospitality, or *omotenashi*, made me feel welcomed everywhere I went.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. I returned home with a camera full of photos and a heart full of memories. The blend of old and new, the quiet reverence, and the bustling streets created an experience unlike any other. If you haven't visited yet, put it at the top of your list. You won't regret it.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.08225013}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.02000814}, {"token": "asing", "logprob": -0.8875865}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -4.5181343e-05}, {"token": " Bloss", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": 